                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.77 Chinese Eastern/293: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >MacMurray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-09-02T13:00:00+08:00">September 2, 1929—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received September 2—9:21 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>783. My 782, September 1, noon. Following from Reuter, Shanghai, September 1st:
                        <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“The Foreign Minister Dr. C. T. Wang, commenting on reports from Moscow
                            regarding the joint Sino-Russian declaration for a settlement of the
                            Chinese Eastern Railway dispute, makes the following statement: ‘The
                            National Government can find no reason to replace the present chairman
                            of the directorate of the railway with a new chairman. A report from
                            certain quarters implying that China has agreed or would agree to such a
                            proposal from the Soviet is absolutely groundless. The question has
                            never been raised and should Russia put it forward the National
                            Government would find it impossible to accept the demand.’”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">MacMurray</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
